              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F65</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$200 (14%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,599.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,499.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 (6%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F83</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,799.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 (6%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F85</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$200 (9%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">C80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,399.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,099.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$300 (13%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">F89</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,899.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,699.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$200 (7%)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The F65 stands out: its $200 cut puts it only $100 above the F63, which makes the stronger motor and 350 lb
            rating cheap to add. Percentages are rounded. A struck-through price tells you what Sole calls regular, not
            what most buyers pay, so compare against the price on other days rather than the saving shown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Sole vs Horizon and NordicTrack at the Same Money</h2>
<p>
            Sole&rsquo;s closest rivals on price are Horizon, which also sells without a subscription, and NordicTrack, which
            builds around iFIT. Using the Amazon prices from our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-60-inch-deck/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">60-inch treadmill roundup</a> (21 November 2026):
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Around $1,000:</strong> the Horizon 7.0 AT at about $935 against the Sole F63 at $1,099.99. Both
            have a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph and a 325 lb rating. The Horizon is cheaper and adds Apple Watch support; the
            Sole states a 3.0 HP motor and no subscription either way.</li>
            <li><strong>Around $1,500:</strong> the Horizon 7.4 AT at about $1,500 against the Sole F80 at $1,499.99. The
            Horizon has the wider 22-inch deck and an included chest strap; the Sole has a 10.1-inch touchscreen.</li>
            <li><strong>Around $2,000:</strong> the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 at $1,999 against the Sole F85 at $1,999.99.
            Both are 22 by 60 inches. The NordicTrack adds decline and iFIT classes at $39 a month; the Sole has no
            subscription.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/sole-vs-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole vs NordicTrack</a> comparison goes further.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="per-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cost per Workout Over Five Years</h2>
<p>
            Because there is no membership, a Sole&rsquo;s cost per workout falls steadily the longer you use it. At three
            workouts a week for five years (780 workouts):
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>F50:</strong> $899.99, about $1.15 a workout.</li>
            <li><strong>F63:</strong> $1,099.99, about $1.41 a workout.</li>
            <li><strong>F80:</strong> $1,499.99, about $1.92 a workout.</li>
            <li><strong>F85:</strong> $1,999.99, about $2.56 a workout.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Add delivery upgrades, a mat and any repairs outside the warranty to get the full figure. Our <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home treadmill vs gym membership</a> guide sets these numbers against a gym.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extras" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Price Does Not Include</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>In-room delivery and assembly.</strong> Standard delivery is curbside; the upgrade is priced at
            checkout.</li>
            <li><strong>A mat.</strong> Worth it on carpet or upstairs; see <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill mats</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>A suitable outlet.</strong> Treadmills draw a lot of power; follow Sole&rsquo;s manual on circuits
            and extension leads.</li>
            <li><strong>Lubricant and upkeep.</strong> Small, but regular; our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance</a> guide lists what to budget for.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a Sole Treadmill Worth the Price?</h2>
<p>
            Sole costs more at the start than many budget brands, and less over five years than most class-led ones. What
            you are paying for, on Sole&rsquo;s own pages, is a lifetime frame and motor warranty, 60-inch decks from the F63
            upward, and no subscription. What you are not getting at the lower end is a built-in screen; the F63 and F65
            pair with your own device instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            A Sole makes the most sense if you run several times a week, plan to keep the treadmill for years and do not
            want a monthly bill. It makes less sense if you mainly walk a few times a week, where the F50&rsquo;s price, or a
            cheaper walking treadmill, is plenty, or if trainer-led classes are the main reason you are buying, in which
            case an iFIT or Peloton machine builds them in.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before buying, ask Sole for the parts and labour terms in writing, since the product pages we checked state only
            the lifetime frame and motor cover. Those terms decide most repair bills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Used Sole</h2>
<p>
            Sole treadmills come up second-hand often, because they are heavy and people move house. A used one can save a
            lot against the new prices above, but check a few things first:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The model and year.</strong> Find the label on the frame and compare it with the current range; an
            older model may have a different motor, deck or console.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty transfer.</strong> Ask Sole whether the warranty passes to a second owner; many treadmill
            warranties cover the original purchaser only.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt and deck.</strong> Run it, walk on it and look for a worn, glazed or frayed belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Moving it.</strong> Sole treadmills are heavy; budget for help or a mover.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a> lists the wear points to check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Sole Treadmill Is Worth the Money?</h2>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">If you…</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Buy</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price</th></tr></thead>
